TypeScript looks simple when you're writing basic types and interfaces.But once you start exploring its advanced features, you realize it can do much more than just check whether a variable is a string or a number.
Some TypeScript features can help you catch mistakes at compile time, build smarter APIs, and even calculate types based on other types.
Here are 8 TypeScript tricks that made me appreciate its type system a lot more.
typeof


What if you could create a TypeScript type directly from a JavaScript object instead of maintaining both separately?
You can.
const config = {
  environment: "production",
  retries: 3,
  debug: false,
};

type Config = typeof config;

Now Config represents the structure inferred from config.
But there is an important detail: environment is inferred as string, not specifically "production".
If you want to preserve literal values, use as const:
const config = {
  environment: "production",
  retries: 3,
  debug: false,
} as const;

type Config = typeof config;

Now the properties have literal types, and they become readonly in the inferred type.
Why this is useful: Configuration objects, shared constants, and avoiding duplicate type definitions.
Imagine you have a list of supported payment methods.
You want the list to be the source of truth, rather than maintaining a separate union type.
const paymentMethods = [
  "card",
  "paypal",
  "bank-transfer",
] as const;

type PaymentMethod =
  (typeof paymentMethods)[number];

The resulting type is:
type PaymentMethod =
  "card" | "paypal" | "bank-transfer";

Let's break it down:
typeof paymentMethods gets the array's type.
[number] extracts the type of its elements.
as const preserves each string as a literal type.
Now this works:
const method: PaymentMethod = "card";

But this fails:
const method: PaymentMethod = "crypto";
// TypeScript error

Why this is useful: Roles, permissions, status values, supported features, and configuration options.
